  "content": {
    "id": "p37-q15-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "15",
    "prompt": "At a party with $n$ guests, everyone shakes hands. Prove that there is a pair of guests who shake hands with the same number of people. Hint: Label the guests with the number of hands they shake.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "The counts $0$ and $n-1$ cannot both occur. Thus $n$ guests occupy at most $n-1$ handshake-count categories, so two guests have the same count.",
      "worked": "Label each guest with the number of other guests they shake hands with. The possible labels are $0,1,\\ldots,n-1$.\n\nIf someone has label $0$, nobody can have label $n-1$, since a guest who shakes hands with everyone would also shake hands with that person. Hence these two labels cannot both occur.\n\nThere are therefore at most $n-1$ available labels for $n$ guests. By the pigeonhole principle, at least two guests have the same label, so they shake hands with the same number of people.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 48
  },
